30
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.200
We have our own internal code review tool,

31
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.880
which we've also released out externally.

32
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:42.360
And we have our own internal agent that does PR updates.

33
00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.800
So hey, you push a PR up, you got a code review back.

34
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.520
You then want to address it and push an update up.

35
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:49.520
And then you get another code review back and iterate.

36
00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:53.040
And so we woke up one morning and we have a PR that's burned about $600,

37
00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:57.080
because both those agents were doing exactly what we had told them to do.

38
00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.320
The PR update agent was, you know, updating and response

39
00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.720
to PR comments, and the review agent was reviewing those.

40
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:07.840
And there wasn't this concept of like, hey, this is far enough, or hey,

41
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.160
the scope of this ticket is exactly just targeting this.

42
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:12.280
And maybe this thing that you

43
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:14.960
found in a related code file that happened to end up in your context

44
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:16.440
because one line was changed.

45
00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:18.480
Maybe file a ticket or do something else there.

46
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.640
These are kind of like built in processes and built in assumptions and built

47
00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.520
in just ways of doing engineering work as humans that we have learned over time

48
00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.280
that these agents don't know because we give them a task, update the PR

49
00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.360
and another task, go review the PR and then they'll just go do that.

50
00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:37.520
And so that layer, that context, that intent, that kind of

51
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.120
outside of the agent layer, that's where this issue arose,

52
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.280
because agents were doing what we told them to do.

53
00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:46.240
But then also where we tried to fix it, rather than trying to say, like, hey,

54
00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:49.880
we need to build into the harness this assumption of like how you do better

55
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:51.680
code review or how you push back.

56
00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.680
It's like, no, let's change the way that we are constructing the system.

57
00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.600
So when I think about kind of like what the factory is

58
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:04.040
and how we deliver software, we've got kind of like five stages here.

59
00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:06.040
One is what is a useful task.

60
00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.600
So this is work that is valuable to us as a company,

61
00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.080
either in terms of experimentation or in terms of product

62
00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:15.400
work, or in terms of any work that a software engineer does to

63
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.760
what is the shared context

64
00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.160
in the working environment that this task is being done in the shared context

65
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:21.880
is everything within our

66
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:23.520
the environment is something that has spun up

67
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.840
with our dev dependencies, with tests, with creds,

68
00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.440
so they can actually test and exercise and verify.

69
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.960
Then there's the agent execution bit. That bit's

70
00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.800
Pretty dead simple.

71
00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.960
We give it a dev sandbox and we run it in YOLO mode on that sandbox.

72
00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.560
And we want to audit.

73
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.320
And then we get things out at the other end.

74
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.880
Then there's the verification and delivery. Cool,

75
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.080
I've got this PR or I've got this code out.

76
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.360
How do I then trust that that is good or good enough or to whatever quality bar

77
00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:48.200
without kind of

78
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.520
degrading into these cycles that we saw with a review agent earlier on.

79
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.880
And then finally, and I think most importantly,

80
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.920
how do I make this loop better the next time?

81
00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:57.280
And so what is the feedback

