245
00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.840
How do we evaluate it?

246
00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.280
And then frankly, how do we then track that against production.

247
00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:36.680
So if we have changes that have caused the production incident,

248
00:11:36.680 --> 00:11:39.960
yeah, we 100% should go look back at the traces of those changes.

249
00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:42.440
We should go look back at that PR review cycle.

250
00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:44.960
We should go look back at how the agent went and did that.

251
00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:47.440
And we can, because, you know, we're storing these agent transcripts

252
00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.680
and we're seeing into basically the engineer's head and the decisions

253
00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:54.480
that were made that led to a production out, a production outage.

254
00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.680
And so that then gives us a very interesting place to mine,

255
00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:00.120
because if we're investing in that, it's outside of the agent,

256
00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:02.560
but it allows us to create a verification structure

257
00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:05.360
or additional context or additional environment

258
00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:08.840
or something else that gives us confidence that this won't happen again

259
00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:09.560
in the future.

260
00:12:09.560 --> 00:12:13.040
And not just a test, because agents are really good at either faking tests

261
00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:16.040
or getting around tests, or just writing lots and lots of tests.

262
00:12:16.040 --> 00:12:16.640
But whatever.

263
00:12:16.640 --> 00:12:19.920
That higher level invariant that we actually care about is because

264
00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:23.320
the production outage isn't strictly like agent did the wrong thing.

265
00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:24.320
It's normally...

266
00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:24.680
It can be.

267
00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:28.000
Sometimes there's just bugs, but it's normally it violated an invariant

268
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.440
that was implied somewhere else in the system that then caused data

269
00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:34.560
to go bad, or something else as it flows through to go wrong.

270
00:12:36.920 --> 00:12:37.880
Cool.

271
00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.960
In terms of kind of that feedback loop, and now we're getting back into kind of

272
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:41.480
what I think

273
00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:44.840
is one of the more powerful aspects and where kind of the investment should be

274
00:12:44.960 --> 00:12:48.080
is in terms of what that next task kind of inherits.

275
00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.960
So this is an example that we had early on in building.

276
00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:54.920
We were constantly churning on our queue.

277
00:12:54.920 --> 00:12:57.120
So we use a durable work queue basically.

278
00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:57.960
Right.

279
00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:01.800
You write a queue item into it once there's only one singleton of that.

280
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.000
So it can't be picked up multiple times.

281
00:13:04.000 --> 00:13:05.920
And we can track kind of the transition of state

282
00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:09.400
through multiple systems as that work as that item gets worked on.

283
00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:11.480
We were constantly stepping on our toes.

284
00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:14.200
And by that I mean agents were constantly stepping on their toes.

285
00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:15.360
So we fix a bug,

286
00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.720
then another agent would introduce a bug, and then we'd fix it again,

287
00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:20.320
and then it would get introduced and it was just a mess.

288
00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:22.760
It was the part of our system that being core to

289
00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:25.160
the system was being changed the most in the early days,

290
00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.520
and also the part of the system that had the most bugs

291
00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:32.360
and therefore the most agent time spent fixing them.

292
00:13:32.440 --> 00:13:35.200
And so what we did is we built a formal model of it.

293
00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:38.840
So we used Quint and built a formal model of kind of like the basic queue behavior

294
00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:42.200
in terms of putting stuff on, having stuff worked on, taking stuff

295
00:13:42.200 --> 00:13:45.200
off and verifying every single interaction associated with that.

296
00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:47.280
Not something I would have been able to do in a pre-agent world.

297
00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:49.040
Like, I know what formal modeling is.

298
00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:50.440
I wouldn't be able to do it

299
00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:53.400
without sitting down with the textbook for a very long time.

300
00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:56.400
Very easy to go sit down with Opus or Fable and go do that.

301
00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:00.280
So that verification then meant that this part of the system

302
00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.920
has been completely stable since we introduced it.

303
00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:04.320
Mind you, it's not the whole system.

304
00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.120
It's very small sub part of it, but it's a very small subpart that impacts

305
00:14:08.120 --> 00:14:10.240
every single thing downstream of it.

306
00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:12.040
By no means is our system bug free.

307
00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:14.200
We constantly have bugs that are entirely agent-written,

308
00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:15.520
and we're trying to figure that out.

309
00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:17.560
Like that's part of the learning process for us.

310
00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:19.200
But this part of that has been solid.

311
00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.560
And so by investing in the verification of it, by investing in how we're testing

312
00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.560
and making sure that this code is correct,

313
00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:27.560
we were able to knock out an entire class of bugs.

314
00:14:27.560 --> 00:14:30.800
And agents are really good at working against an optimization target.

315
00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.960
You say, hey, you need to satisfy this held out constraint.

316
00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:35.520
Go figure it out.

317
00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.600
They will happily go bang their heads against the wall

318
00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:40.440
until they have something that satisfies that constraint.

319
00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:42.000
And if you're confident, which you should be

320
00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:45.760
because it's formally verified that that constraint is going to hold, well,

321
00:14:45.760 --> 00:14:48.080
then you've got a great thing to go bang the agent's head against the wall.

322
00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:49.400
That isn't just a unit test.

323
00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:52.600
Cool.

324
00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:56.360
In terms of kind of like the metrics and how we're thinking about, like,

325
00:14:56.360 --> 00:14:58.000
I talk a lot about success in terms

326
00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.920
of making the system better or removing bugs or what does that mean?

327
00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:05.960
Well, there's we've hit this issue as well

328
00:15:05.960 --> 00:15:10.160
in terms of prior to investing in like a deeper understanding of the system.

329
00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:14.720
And so the key point is like if we just look at run success

330
00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:19.640
and run success here for us is agents set up PR, CI passes.

331
00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:20.920
Well then great.

332
00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:22.640
We can swap out between different models,

333
00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:25.680
between different harnesses, between different approaches.

334
00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:27.440
This is to this is an example

335
00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:30.160
from one of the experiments we did looking at effort levels.

336
00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:33.160
Specifically, this was done with the Claude class of models.

337
00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:36.240
And we first just looked at run success.

338
00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:38.920
So are we passing CI at the end of it.

339
00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:40.680
And high effort costs us more.

340
00:15:40.680 --> 00:15:43.000
It's high effort. Medium effort is cheaper.

341
00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:45.640
And we were seeing equivalence across those two

342
00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:48.760
only by kind of then going and looking level deeper looking at

343
00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:54.520
first review approval rate did we see, oh, high effort

344
00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:58.440
Had a higher first approval rate and medium effort had a lower

345
00:15:58.520 --> 00:15:59.360
first approval rate.

346
00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:01.080
What this means is that it wasn't

347
00:16:01.080 --> 00:16:04.520
just we were just looking initially at the cost of just the implementation.

348
00:16:04.840 --> 00:16:07.080
We had to take into account the cost of the whole system, though,

349
00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.040
because we also pay for review.

350
00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:12.760
And so a higher first review approval rate means review is cheaper because

351
00:16:12.760 --> 00:16:13.520
we don't have to pay for

352
00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:15.960
review rounds.

353
00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:18.360
And so only by starting to break out

354
00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:20.120
kind of the different components of our system,

355
00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:21.400
rather than just focusing on

356
00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:23.480
like the coding agent or focusing on the thing

357
00:16:23.480 --> 00:16:25.240
that was actually doing the implementation.

358
00:16:25.240 --> 00:16:26.400
Are we able to start to pick

359
00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:30.200
and decouple and understand where these different interactions are happening?

360
00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:33.960
If we had kind of just looked at this and shipped, the medium effort

361
00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:37.240
or cost would have actually gone up, despite seeing that the headline

362
00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:40.480
costs, the thing that we are focusing on and optimizing for would have gone down.

363
00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:45.840
And I guess mainly the takeaway here is metrics are important.

364
00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:49.120
I don't think that's surprising to anyone, but it's so easy to say yes.

365
00:16:49.120 --> 00:16:50.280
I know metrics are important.

366
00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:53.520
Observability is important, but I think with agents things move so.

367
00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.760
fast and it is hard to kind of pay attention to this system.

368
00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:01.000
To the question earlier about like, how do we have human observability

369
00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:02.000
or human loop?

370
00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.080
We try to put it in by having observability at the meta layer,

371
00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:08.280
by understanding how the whole system overall

372
00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:12.120
is working through throughput or through latency or through other aspects.

373
00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.240
This is more of an example here.

374
00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:21.000
I think, in terms of the interaction mode and the focus on there,

375
00:17:21.040 --> 00:17:25.160
where you can get ROI by thinking less about the agents and more about

376
00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:26.760
how you're interacting with the agent.

377
00:17:26.760 --> 00:17:29.240
This is my example here.

378
00:17:29.240 --> 00:17:31.720
I asked an agent in Slack.

379
00:17:31.720 --> 00:17:33.840
Hey, cool. I've got this PR.

380
00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:35.400
It's stuck in CI.

381
00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:37.840
Why is it taking so long in CI?

382
00:17:37.840 --> 00:17:40.360
Agent came back was saying cool median or sorry

383
00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:43.560
P90 for CI is 15.5 minutes.

384
00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.680
Let's go explore a bunch of ways that we can improve CI.

385
00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:49.760
We also have a merge queue.

386
00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.760
And so that means that when we want to merge it's not just CI.

387
00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:56.120
It needs to wait in the back of a queue and slowly segment and go up.

388
00:17:56.560 --> 00:17:58.080
We do we do groupings.

389
00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.240
Where a couple of PRs land together.

390
00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:03.680
It's to deal with the issue that we had where CI would run effectively

391
00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:06.000
on a branch, but then when it merged into main,

392
00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.120
main had already moved and we'd introduce bugs.

393
00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:11.160
So pretty standard reasons when I push back on

394
00:18:11.200 --> 00:18:14.160
that agent and was like, cool, okay, but what about the merge queue?

395
00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:15.240
How is that impacting it?

396
00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:19.280
So the merge queue itself was 52.4 minutes at P90.

397
00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:22.880
That means roughly we were spending 35 minutes sitting in the queue

398
00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:25.880
waiting and then only 15 minutes on CI.

399
00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:27.960
Can you tell me about the distribution?

400
00:18:27.960 --> 00:18:30.640
So you may have P90 at 15 minutes,

401
00:18:30.640 --> 00:18:35.200
but the sort of 91% might be sort of hours and hours and hours.

402
00:18:35.200 --> 00:18:36.640
Do you know what the distribution of.

403
00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:39.320
I don't have the distribution to hand at the moment.

404
00:18:39.320 --> 00:18:42.720
I believe the max that we saw for merge

405
00:18:42.720 --> 00:18:45.760
queue was like two, 2.5 hours kind of thing.

406
00:18:46.640 --> 00:18:48.440
So long.

407
00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:52.120
Because if you have a priority queue, you've got a power

408
00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:55.320
law, whereas if it's random, you get an exponential.

409
00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:55.760
Yeah.

410
00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:59.760
So the way that our queue works, it's, it's there's no prioritization assigned.

411
00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:03.720
It's basically just whatever lands like whatever gets merged next.

412
00:19:03.880 --> 00:19:05.320
So it's just time series.

413
00:19:06.280 --> 00:19:09.920
And I think where most of our issues were coming around

414
00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:14.280
was that we weren't doing a large enough grouping on the stuff that was landing.

415
00:19:14.280 --> 00:19:17.400
And so we were basically constraining ourselves artificially

416
00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:20.520
by saying we were only landing five things at a time,

417
00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:22.800
five things at a time, with five groups in parallel,

418
00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:26.320
when we could be scaling that out both vertically and horizontally.

419
00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:31.840
So when you measured this roughly how many autumns did you did you measure?

420
00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:32.280
Yeah.

421
00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.360
So our max queue length hit

422
00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:39.720
60 to 80 in that range.

423
00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:43.480
And we looked at kind of over two weeks of data.

424
00:19:44.400 --> 00:19:48.640
I think that's going to be roughly 1,500 plus

425
00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:50.280
PRs. Yeah.

426
00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:53.280
So a decent chunk.

427
00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:55.240
And so I think

428
00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:58.360
kind of the thing for me here, though, is that,

429
00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:01.360
thinking through like

430
00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:04.360
the question that I given the agent in the first place was poorly specified.

431
00:20:04.440 --> 00:20:07.400
Like, what I really cared about was the merge queue I had asked about.

432
00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:11.200
Kind of CI, though, because I was annoyed by CI at that point in time,

433
00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:15.000
and so only by kind of iterating with the agent in that public surface

434
00:20:15.040 --> 00:20:18.720
in Slack, was I able to get to kind of the number that I did right there.

435
00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:22.480
And so a smarter agent, I don't think would have been able to read my mind

436
00:20:22.480 --> 00:20:25.560
and say, hey, you're actually concerned about merge queue

437
00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:28.640
when I'm specifically asking about CI duration.

438
00:20:28.960 --> 00:20:31.800
And so in that case, it's like, cool.

439
00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:33.160
What is the investment there?

440
00:20:33.160 --> 00:20:36.440
Like we might think that, oh, we're going to be saved by kind of smarter agents.

441
00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:38.920
They're going to just solve our problems in that regard.

442
00:20:38.920 --> 00:20:41.920
But if we're asking for poorly specified things, then yeah,

443
00:20:41.960 --> 00:20:44.880
you're going to get some random results coming out at the end of it.

444
00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.760
And so at least the decision that we've made in that I try to kind of embody

445
00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:50.160
in this regard is cool.

446
00:20:50.160 --> 00:20:52.120
Well then let's do that in a public setting.

447
00:20:52.120 --> 00:20:54.400
In Slack, where I can have that conversation

448
00:20:54.400 --> 00:20:57.520
and other folks can chime in in terms of helping me specify

449
00:20:57.520 --> 00:20:59.720
my poorly specified question that I didn't know

450
00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:01.760
was even poorly specified in the first place.

451
00:21:01.760 --> 00:21:04.320
And then it becomes a searchable archive of information

452
00:21:04.320 --> 00:21:05.960
that we can have publicly available.

453
00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:08.200
So now that's context, not context in the repo,

454
00:21:08.200 --> 00:21:11.200
but it's context on Slack that can then feed other agent workflows.

455
00:21:11.320 --> 00:21:14.920
And so that shift in terms of hey, the interaction mode needs to change

456
00:21:14.920 --> 00:21:18.480
in terms of how you're communicating with it to make it more amenable

457
00:21:18.520 --> 00:21:23.000
to further context generation or further context consumption, or more of that

458
00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:27.320
repeated iterative multiplayer flow, I think has benefits this one.

459
00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:28.160
No idea how to measure.

460
00:21:28.160 --> 00:21:31.200
To be clear, I think it's better, but I have no idea how to measure

461
00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:33.080
whether it's making us more efficient or not.

462
00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:36.680
To be talking to the agents in Slack rather than personally on local CLIs.

463
00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:40.720
Cool.

464
00:21:40.720 --> 00:21:44.800
We also think about evidence and so evidence here.

465
00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:48.040
This is where a lot of our ROI kind of stuff comes from.

466
00:21:48.200 --> 00:21:51.200
And frankly, it's on the second to minute timescale.

467
00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:55.160
So a lot of this stuff that is easy to measure is test types.

468
00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:58.160
Lint does a pass, CI does it do kind of the basic things.

469
00:21:58.160 --> 00:21:58.920
That's trivial

470
00:21:58.920 --> 00:22:02.120
because I can run a machine in order to go evaluate it deterministically.

471
00:22:02.240 --> 00:22:04.440
That's not the interesting bit though.

472
00:22:04.440 --> 00:22:05.640
Like what I really care about

473
00:22:05.640 --> 00:22:09.440
is customer outcomes incidents, how much the PR needs to be reworked.

474
00:22:09.720 --> 00:22:13.960
And so the way that we try to think about ROI kind of across this time series or

475
00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:20.000
this time scale is these super expensive figuring out the correlation

476
00:22:20.000 --> 00:22:23.760
between these much less expensive than just measuring this on its own.

477
00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:27.600
And so a lot of the kind of thinking that we do around signals is really

478
00:22:27.640 --> 00:22:31.720
what signals can we derive to think about kind of second cements

479
00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:34.720
that is correlated to minutes, hours, that is correlated to days to weeks.

480
00:22:34.840 --> 00:22:38.560
And we throw off a ton of this data just naturally as an engineering

481
00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:42.320
organization, like we have incidents, we know where those incidents came from,

482
00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:45.480
but then we don't always connect back to the actual agent sessions

483
00:22:45.480 --> 00:22:47.760
that created those incidents or the tests that we're missing, or the link

484
00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:48.560
that was missing,

485
00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:50.480
or the verification function that was missing

486
00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:53.000
that allowed for that incident that happened in the first place.

487
00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:56.400
And so our bet, my bet, is more so

488
00:22:57.080 --> 00:23:01.040
that by focusing on the correlation between these, in terms of figuring out

489
00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:04.520
what bits of tests help lead to better

490
00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:07.960
or rather less review rounds that lead to less

491
00:23:07.960 --> 00:23:11.080
incidents is going to be more valuable to you as an organization,

